<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183168> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Data assimilation technique refers to the method or process used to integrate observational data into a computational model to improve its accuracy and predictive capability. This technique is crucial in fields such as meteorology, oceanography, and environmental science, where it helps to combine real-world measurements with model simulations to produce more reliable and precise results. Describing this property in a research paper highlights the specific approach or algorithm employed to assimilate data, which can be a significant contribution to advancing modeling and forecasting methodologies." .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183168> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Data assimilation technique" .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183169> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Predicate> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183169> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183169> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Assimilated model refers to a computational or mathematical model that has been improved or refined by integrating observational data through data assimilation techniques. This property highlights the use of data assimilation to enhance model accuracy and reliability in the research." .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183169> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Assimilated model" .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183170> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Predicate> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183170> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183170> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "This property describes the specific state variables within a system or model that are updated as part of the research contribution. It highlights which variables' values are changed or evolved during the process or experiment, indicating the dynamic aspects addressed by the study." .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183170> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "state variables updated" .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183171> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Predicate> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183171> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183171> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The 'purpose of assimilation' predicate captures the main goal or objective behind the process of assimilation discussed in a research paper. It describes why assimilation is performed or studied, highlighting the intended outcomes, motivations, or aims that drive the assimilation process within the context of the research. This property helps clarify the contribution of the paper by specifying the targeted purpose or rationale for assimilation." .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183171> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "purpose of assimilation" .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183172> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://orkg.org/orkg/class/Predicate> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183172> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183172> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Assimilation window refers to the specific time period during which observational data is integrated into a model or system to improve its accuracy and predictive capability. It is a critical parameter in data assimilation processes, determining the temporal scope for incorporating new information to update model states or forecasts." .
<http://orkg.org/orkg/predicate/P183172> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "assimilation window" .
